# Component Architecture (Frontend)

## Purpose

Consistent UI layer structure for React, Next.js, and similar frameworks.

## Component Types

| Type | Role | Location |
|------|------|----------|
| Page/Route | Data loading orchestration, layout | `app/`, `pages/`, `routes/` |
| Feature | Domain-specific UI + hooks | `features/<name>/` |
| Shared UI | Design system, primitives | `components/ui/` |
| Layout | Shell, nav, providers | `components/layout/` |

## Rules

- **Presentational vs container**: dumb components receive props; data fetching in pages/hooks.
- Extract reusable logic to **custom hooks** (`useX`), not copy-paste in components.
- Colocate: `FeatureName.tsx`, `FeatureName.test.tsx`, `useFeatureName.ts`, `types.ts`.
- Prefer composition over prop drilling; use context sparingly for truly global state.

## Component Size

- Target < 150 lines per component file.
- Split when mixing: fetch + form + table + modal in one file.

## Props

- Explicit typed props interface: `FeatureNameProps`.
- Avoid `{...rest}` spread onto DOM without filtering.
- Default exports only if project convention; otherwise named exports.

## Server vs Client (Next.js App Router)

- Server Components by default; `'use client'` only when needed (events, hooks, browser APIs).
- Do not fetch secrets in client components.
- Pass serializable props across server/client boundary.

## Example Structure

```
features/checkout/
  CheckoutPage.tsx
  CheckoutForm.tsx
  useCheckout.ts
  checkout.types.ts
  index.ts              # public exports only
```